 "about_text": "Founded in 1954 within Barnsdall Art Park, the Los Angeles Municipal Art Gallery is the longest continuously running institution in Los Angeles devoted solely to exhibiting art. Operated by the city's Department of Cultural Affairs, it grew out of the city-led All City Outdoor Art Festivals and Kenneth Ross's vision for decentralized civic arts programming. The gallery focuses on artists connected to Los Angeles, with particular attention to underrepresented and emerging voices, alongside mid-career and established practitioners. Past exhibitors who later gained wider recognition include Carlos Almaraz, John Baldessari, Vija Celmins, David Hammons, Barbara Kruger, Kerry James Marshall, and Ed Ruscha. Admission and programs are free, and the gallery does not maintain a permanent collection.",
